.Im MicroEmacs macro;function;string
.so me.tni
.TH &blen 4
.XI &blen - "String length in bytes"
.XI &bmid - "Mid string in bytes"
.XI &batoi - "First byte to integer"
.XI &bitoa - "Integer to byte"
.XI &bhex - "String bytes to hexadecimal"
.XI &hexb - "Hexadecimal to string bytes"
.SH NAME
&blen, &bmid, &batoi, &bitoa, &bhex, &hexb \- Byte functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.na
.B &blen
.I str
.br
.B &bmid
.I str index len
.br
.B &batoi
.I str
.br
.B &bitoa
.I num
.br
.B &bhex
.I str
.br
.B &hexb
.I hex
.ad
.SH DESCRIPTION
The byte functions work on the bytes of a string, which are the bytes the string would be written to a file as: in a
Unicode build the UTF-8 bytes of each character, an invalid byte being that byte and a Nul character the byte
\fC0\fR. In other builds a byte is a character.
.PP
Bytes made into a string are decoded as a whole, in a Unicode build valid UTF-8 becomes characters, any other byte
an invalid byte and \fC0\fR the Nul character.
.IP "\fB&blen\fR \fIstr\fR"
Returns the number of bytes in \fIstr\fR.
.IP "\fB&bmid\fR \fIstr index len\fR"
Returns the \fIlen\fR bytes of \fIstr\fR from byte \fIindex\fR (the first byte is 0), negative values work as for
.Ht &mid 4 .
A character may be split, its bytes are then invalid bytes.
.IP "\fB&batoi\fR \fIstr\fR"
Returns the value of the first byte of \fIstr\fR, \fC0\fR if it is empty.
.IP "\fB&bitoa\fR \fInum\fR"
Returns the byte \fInum\fR as a string, in a Unicode build a byte from \fC0x80\fR is an invalid byte.
.IP "\fB&bhex\fR \fIstr\fR"
Returns the bytes of \fIstr\fR in lower case hexadecimal, e.g. \fC&bhex\ "a\\xe9"\fR returns "\fC61c3a9\fR" in a
Unicode build.
.IP "\fB&hexb\fR \fIhex\fR"
Returns the string of the bytes given in hexadecimal by \fIhex\fR, it fails if \fIhex\fR holds a non-hexadecimal
character or half a byte.
.SH NOTES
As each result is decoded on its own, joining the results of \fB&bitoa\fR gives invalid bytes rather than a UTF-8
character, \fC&bmid\fR of the joined string decodes them together:
.CS
&bmid &cat &bitoa 0xc3 &bitoa 0xa9 0 2
.CE
returns an 'e' acute (U+00E9) in a Unicode build, as does \fC&hexb\ "c3a9"\fR.
.PP
\fC&blen\ &lef\ @wl\ $window-col\fR gives the byte offset of the cursor in the current line.
.SH EXAMPLE
The following converts a string to its bytes in hexadecimal and back:
.Me $emf-scheme
.CS
set-variable #l0 &bhex "Caf\\xe9"
set-variable #l1 &hexb #l0
.CE
.Me $ehf-scheme
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.na
